The node already has links to both sides, so there is no need to search for it. Save its previous and next nodes, then connect those two nodes to each other. If one side is missing, the node is at an end of the list.

```cpp
/**
class ListNode
{
 * Definition for doubly-linked list.
 *  public:
 *      int data;
 *      ListNode *prev;
 *      ListNode *next;
 *      ListNode() : data(0), prev(nullptr), next(nullptr) {}
 *      ListNode(int x) : data(x), prev(nullptr), next(nullptr) {}
 *      ListNode(int x, ListNode *prev, ListNode *next) : data(x), prev(prev), next(next) {}
};
*/

class Solution {
public:
    void deleteGivenNode(ListNode *node) {
        if (node == nullptr) {
            return;
        }

        if (node->prev != nullptr) {
            node->prev->next = node->next;
        }

        if (node->next != nullptr) {
            node->next->prev = node->prev;
        }

        node->prev = nullptr;
        node->next = nullptr;
    }
};
```

For example, in `1 <-> 2 <-> 3`, deleting `2` changes `1->next` to `3` and `3->prev` to `1`.

Time complexity: `O(1)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`. The input links are changed, and the removed node is detached.

This version handles the two links separately. The previous node skips over the target, and the next node points back to the previous node. The checks also make the method safe for a single-node list or a null reference.

```java
/*
// Definition for a Node.
class ListNode {
    public int data;
    public ListNode prev;
    public ListNode next;
    public ListNode();
    public ListNode(int data);
    public ListNode(int data, ListNode prev, ListNode next);
};
*/

class Solution {
    public void deleteGivenNode(ListNode node) {
        if (node == null) {
            return;
        }

        ListNode left = node.prev;
        ListNode right = node.next;

        if (left == null && right == null) {
            return;
        }

        if (left == null) {
            right.prev = null;
        } else {
            left.next = right;
        }

        if (right == null) {
            left.next = null;
        } else {
            right.prev = left;
        }

        node.prev = null;
        node.next = null;
    }
}
```

A target in the middle updates both neighbors. If the target is the tail, only the previous node needs its `next` link cleared. Since the method receives only the node, an external head variable cannot be changed when the target is the head.

Time: `O(1)`.
Space: `O(1)`.

This uses a slightly different trick when the target has a next node. Its data is replaced with the next node's data, and that next node is removed. For a tail node, the target itself is unlinked directly.

This keeps the target object in the list, so it is useful when the caller only cares about the list values. It should not be used when object identity of every remaining node matters.

```javascript
/**
 * // Definition for a Node.
 * function ListNode(data, prev, next) {
 *    this.data = data;
 *    this.prev = prev || null;
 *    this.next = next || null;
 * };
 */

class Solution {
  /**
   *
   * @param {ListNode} node
   */
  deleteGivenNode(node) {
    if (node === null || node === undefined) {
      return;
    }

    if (node.next !== null) {
      const replacement = node.next;
      node.data = replacement.data;
      node.next = replacement.next;

      if (replacement.next !== null) {
        replacement.next.prev = node;
      }

      replacement.prev = null;
      replacement.next = null;
      return;
    }

    if (node.prev !== null) {
      node.prev.next = null;
    }

    node.prev = null;
  }
}
```

For `2 <-> 5 <-> 8`, deleting the node containing `5` copies `8` into it and removes the old `8` node. When the given node is the tail, no replacement exists, so the previous node is connected to `null`.

Time complexity: `O(1)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`. The links in the input list are changed.

The important part is not to touch a missing neighbor. First handle the left link, then the right link. This makes the same code work for a middle node, the first node, and the last node.

An edge case is a one-node list. Both neighbors are absent, so there is no list link to repair. Clearing the node links is still safe.

```csharp
/*
// Definition for a Node.
public class ListNode {
    public int data;
    public ListNode prev;
    public ListNode next;
    public ListNode();
    public ListNode(int data);
    public ListNode(int data, ListNode prev, ListNode next);
}
*/

public class Solution {
    public void DeleteGivenNode(ListNode node) {
        if (node == null) {
            return;
        }

        if (node.prev == null) {
            if (node.next != null) {
                node.next.prev = null;
            }
        } else {
            node.prev.next = node.next;
        }

        if (node.next == null) {
            if (node.prev != null) {
                node.prev.next = null;
            }
        } else {
            node.next.prev = node.prev;
        }

        node.prev = null;
        node.next = null;
    }
}
```

For a middle node, both neighbor assignments run. For a head node, only the next node's `prev` link is changed. The method cannot return a new head because the required signature returns `void`.

Time complexity: `O(1)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`, with no traversal or extra collection.

